## MECHANISM — PROVEN sess243 (supersedes sess241/242 orphan framing)
NO orphan, NO tracking loss. Root = **trylock sweep is signal-free vs
lazily-cached holders**:
1. Each AG ends cached (lazy EX) at its affine node; cached grants
   release ONLY on BAST (by design).
2. test30 rsync, dirty trans, needs space; own ag=12 lockable (absent
   from P5G sweep) but can't satisfy the grow → sweeps other 24 AGs via
   mxfs_ag_dlm_lock_bounded (xfs_mxfs_dlm.c:36651) = 40×100ms NOQUEUE.
3. NOQUEUE (dlm_caw.c:7994) returns -EAGAIN BEFORE waiter registration
   (8067+) and BEFORE caw_send_bast_mcast (8107) → no on-disk waiters
   bit (holder poll thread skips !slot.waiters, 11589) and no UDP hint
   (bast_recv_fn 11673) → holders never demote → -EAGAIN forever.
4. Pre-5626s BASTs came from OTHER nodes' blocking acquires (fio/dd,
   test16 rsync P1-AGWAIT); once those ended, zero signal sources.

Evidence: 'unlock exhausted' = 0 on test13/test30/test16/test8; P5N NAKs
all disk_held=0 (mcast is broadcast, non-holders NAK = benign); test8
P12-WORK ag=4 COMMIT @5556 + test16 @5621 = holders demote fine when
BASTed; test13's own-ag P5N @5625 disk_held=0 → later bit2 = legit
re-acquire. sess241 "-EAGAIN on ITS OWN ag" REFUTED (ag=12 not in sweep).

## Fix shape (pending RULE-5 consult — IN PROGRESS)
Scope demand signal to the bounded/dirty-grow path (only path with no
blocking fallback): on NOQUEUE conflict send caw_send_bast_mcast hint
(new flag e.g. MXFS_LKF_DEMAND or a _nb_demand wrapper), rate-limited
(1st try + every Nth of the 40). Holder hint→bastq→demote ~100-600ms →
retry succeeds. Flood risk hist: sess128 RcvbufErrors — do NOT hint the
generic allocator first-pass trylock (dialloc has blocking 2nd pass).
Deeper Q for GPT: idle cluster converges to all-AGs-cached → any full-
own-AG dirty grow livelocks; is hint-on-demand enough or need waiter
bit (lossless) / idle decay? Waiter-bit route is fraught (samenode
waiter-cancel family #15).

## RULE-5 ruling DONE (ccmemory ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess243-GPT-ruling-
aglock-livelock-sticky-revoke — READ IT before implementing).
